\section{Convolutional neural networks using Keras}

\textbf{Background reading:} Follow the \href{https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1HC_QN2mCq9bkCPzmkP8RaR3RokFQCWo9oPuU7rFyR8Y/edit?usp=sharing}{general reading instructions for Block 4}.

The random-search guidance is adapted from \citet[Table~11.1 and Section~11.4.4]{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}. The procedure is specified below; no reading of that book is required.

We are now going to implement a convolutional neural network using Keras. Here Ch. 5.1-5.3 in \citet{chollet2018deep} and the \href{https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/blob/master/additional_material/supplementary_r_files/cnn_example_cifar_10.R}{Keras 3 CIFAR-10 example} might be useful for getting started. Remember to load the \texttt{tensorflow} R package before loading the \texttt{keras3} R package.

If you have installed \texttt{keras3}, you can load the MNIST dataset. This dataset contains data on handwritten digits that we want to classify. To access the data, we use \texttt{keras3} as follows.

<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
library(tensorflow)
library(keras3)
mnist <- dataset_mnist()
mnist$train$x <- mnist$train$x/255
mnist$test$x <- mnist$test$x/255
@

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Display training observations 1, 2, and 3, with their true digit labels, and include these images in your report.
<<echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
idx <- 1
im <- mnist$train$x[idx,,]
# Transpose the image
im <- t(apply(im, 2, rev))
image(1:28, 1:28, im, col=gray((0:255)/255), xlab = "", ylab = "",
        xaxt='n', yaxt='n', main=paste(mnist$train$y[idx]))
@

\newpage
\item (1p) Implement a Convolutional Neural Network for the MNIST dataset. The network should have two convolutional layers as follows.
<<>>=
## ______________________________________________________________________
## Layer (type)                     Output Shape                Param #
## ======================================================================
## conv2d (Conv2D)                  (None, 26, 26, 32)          320
## ______________________________________________________________________
## max_pooling2d (MaxPooling2D)     (None, 13, 13, 32)          0
## ______________________________________________________________________
## conv2d (Conv2D)                  (None, 11, 11, 32)          9248
## ______________________________________________________________________
## flatten (Flatten)                (None, 3872)                0
## ______________________________________________________________________
## dense (Dense)                    (None, 64)                  247872
## ______________________________________________________________________
## dense (Dense)                    (None, 10)                  650
## ======================================================================
## Total params: 258,090
## Trainable params: 258,090
## Non-trainable params: 0
@
\begin{exampleblock}{Counting convolutional parameters}
Four $2\times2$ filters applied to three input channels have
\[
4(2\cdot2\cdot3+1)=52
\]
parameters. Each filter spans all input channels, giving $2\cdot2\cdot3$ kernel weights and one bias per filter.
\end{exampleblock}

\item (1p) Explain why there are 320 parameters in the first layer. How many are kernel weights (and why), and how many biases?
\item (1p) Train the network and use \texttt{evaluate()} on the MNIST test set to report its \textbf{test loss and test accuracy}. Include your training code once.
\enlargethispage{3\baselineskip}
\item (3p) Choose and briefly justify at least three hyper-parameters and their sampling distributions. Use random search to train 10 fresh models: for each trial, draw a setting from each chosen distribution and train a newly initialized model using those settings. Keep the training/validation split and number of epochs fixed, and record each model's settings and final-epoch validation accuracy. Select the best setting using validation accuracy, then report the code and test accuracy of your final model. Keep the test data out of the search.

Examples of hyper-parameters you may include are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Learning rate:} sample uniformly on a logarithmic scale between $10^{-4}$ and $10^{-2}$.
\item \textbf{Filters per convolutional layer:} sample uniformly from $\{16,32,64\}$.
\item \textbf{Batch size:} sample uniformly from $\{32,64,128\}$.
\item \textbf{Dense hidden units:} sample uniformly from $\{32,64,128\}$.
\item \textbf{Dropout rate:} sample uniformly between 0 and 0.5, if you include a dropout layer.
\end{itemize}
These are suggestions; other justified choices are also allowed. Adapt the scaffold below to the hyper-parameters you choose.

\begin{exampleblock}{A random-search loop}
This scaffold uses \texttt{keras3}. First prepare fixed training and validation arrays \texttt{x\_train} and \texttt{x\_val} with dimensions \texttt{c(n, 28, 28, 1)}, scaled to $[0,1]$, and matching label vectors with values 0--9. Write \texttt{build\_model(filters, learning\_rate)} to create and compile a fresh CNN using sparse categorical cross-entropy and the supplied settings. The choices below are illustrative; adapt them to your search.
<<random-search-example,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
settings <- data.frame(
  filters = sample(c(16L, 32L, 64L), 10, replace = TRUE),
  learning_rate = 10^runif(10, -4, -2),
  batch_size = sample(c(32L, 64L, 128L), 10, replace = TRUE)
)
settings$val_accuracy <- NA_real_
for (i in seq_len(nrow(settings))) {
  clear_session()
  model <- build_model(settings$filters[i],
                       settings$learning_rate[i])
  history <- fit(model, x_train, y_train,
    validation_data = list(x_val, y_val),
    epochs = 5, batch_size = settings$batch_size[i],
    verbose = 0)
  settings$val_accuracy[i] <-
    tail(history$metrics$val_accuracy, 1)
}
best <- settings[which.max(settings$val_accuracy), ]
best
@
Rebuild the selected configuration and fit it before evaluating it on the test data. Here \texttt{10\^{}runif(10, -4, -2)} samples learning rates uniformly on a logarithmic scale, from $10^{-4}$ to $10^{-2}$.
\end{exampleblock}

\newpage
\item (1p) Load CIFAR-10 using \texttt{dataset\_cifar10()} (see the \href{https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/blob/master/additional_material/supplementary_r_files/cnn_example_cifar_10.R}{Keras 3 CIFAR-10 example}). Visualize one image of your choice together with its class/category.

\emph{Hint!} Display the first unscaled training image as RGB as follows. Change the index 1 to choose another image; use \texttt{max = 1} if you have already scaled the pixels to $[0,1]$.
<<cifar-image-hint,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
cifar10 <- dataset_cifar10()
plot(as.raster(cifar10$train$x[1, , , ], max = 255))
@

\item (1p) Implement the following CNN as your \emph{baseline} for CIFAR-10; reuse it in the comparison below.
<<>>=
## Model: "sequential"
## ______________________________________________________________________
## Layer (type)                     Output Shape                  Param #
## ======================================================================
## conv2d (Conv2D)                  (None, 30, 30, 32)            896
## ______________________________________________________________________
## max_pooling2d (MaxPooling2D)     (None, 15, 15, 32)            0
## ______________________________________________________________________
## conv2d_1 (Conv2D)                (None, 13, 13, 64)            18496
## ______________________________________________________________________
## max_pooling2d_1 (MaxPooling2D)   (None, 6, 6, 64)              0
## ______________________________________________________________________
## conv2d_2 (Conv2D)                (None, 4, 4, 64)              36928
## ______________________________________________________________________
## flatten (Flatten)                (None, 1024)                  0
## ______________________________________________________________________
## dense (Dense)                    (None, 64)                    65600
## ______________________________________________________________________
## dense_1 (Dense)                  (None, 10)                    650
## ======================================================================
## Total params: 122,570
## Trainable params: 122,570
## Non-trainable params: 0
