@
\emph{Note!} This problem is more complex than the MNIST problem, so don't be surprised if you get lower accuracy than you got on the MNIST dataset.

\item (1p) Why do we now have 896 parameters in the first convolutional layer?
\item (2p) Train and compare three CIFAR networks: the baseline above and two variations, each making one motivated change to the baseline (for example, changing the number of filters or adding dropout). Keep the training/validation split, preprocessing, optimizer, learning rate, batch size, and number of epochs fixed. Reuse the baseline run. Include each model's Keras summary and a table of training and validation accuracies at the final epoch. Briefly state your expected effect and explain the observed difference from the baseline; an improvement is not required. Use validation results for the comparison and keep the test data out of model selection. \label{item:three}
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\section{* Implementing a convolutional layer}\label{sec:manual-convolution}

For VG, complete either this section or Section~\ref{sec:transfer-learning}. You may do both, but only one VG point is awarded. Report the time spent on one chosen option.


As a first step, we will implement a layer of a convolutional neural network with one filter. We will here not train the layer, only implement it to understand the inner workings. You are not allowed to use \texttt{convolve()} in R in the final solution, but you can use it to debug your code if you want to.

The convolution explanation is adapted from \citet[Figure~9.1]{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}. All implementation details needed here are given below; no reading of that book is required. The video \citet{ng2017}, listed in the Block 4 reading instructions, provides a visual explanation.

For an $R\times C$ image $X$ and an $m\times m$ kernel $K$, compute each filter response as
\[
S_{ij}=\sum_{u=1}^{m}\sum_{v=1}^{m}X_{i+u-1,j+v-1}K_{uv}.
\]
Slide the kernel one row or column at a time, without padding or flipping the kernel. The result has dimensions $(R-m+1)\times(C-m+1)$. This is the CNN convention, also called cross-correlation. A convolutional layer then applies $\operatorname{activation}(S_{ij}+b)$ separately at every position. For max-pooling, take the maximum in each non-overlapping $2\times2$ window with stride 2, discarding any incomplete border windows.

Start by importing examples from the MNIST dataset as follows.

<<eval = TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("mnist_example")
@

To visualize an image use:

<<eval = FALSE>>=
im <- mnist_example[["4"]]
image(1:ncol(im), 1:nrow(im), im,
      xlab = "", ylab = "",
      xaxt='n', yaxt='n', main="4")
@

\emph{Plotting orientation:} In \texttt{image()}, matrix rows correspond to the $x$-axis and columns to the $y$-axis. Interpret filter directions in the plotted image's coordinates, and use the same plotting convention for the original image and its feature maps.

<<echo = FALSE>>=
convolution <- function(X,K){
  matrix(c(-1, 27,
           -36, -36), byrow = TRUE, ncol = 2)
}

convolutional_layer <- function(X,K,b,activation){
      matrix(c(99,  127,
               64, 64), byrow = TRUE, ncol = 2)
}

maxpool_layer <- function(X){
  matrix(c(250,  144,
           198, 241), byrow = TRUE, ncol = 2)
}
@


\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Visualize the MNIST example digit 4.
\item (3p) Implement a convolution function called \texttt{convolution(X, K)} that takes an input MNIST image (\texttt{X}), an arbitrary large square kernel (\texttt{K}) and returns a feature map. Use stride 1 and no padding (``valid'' convolution). Below is an example of how it should work.
<<>>=
X <- mnist_example[["4"]][12:15,12:15]
X

K <- matrix(c(-0.5, -0.5, -0.5,
              1,  1,  1,
              -0.5, -0.5, -0.5), nrow = 3, byrow = TRUE)
K

convolution(X, K)
@
\item (1p) Visualize the feature map of MNIST example digit 4 using the above three by three kernel \texttt{K}.
\item (3p) Now implement all steps in a \texttt{convolutional\_layer(X, K, b, activation)} function that takes the kernel, bias and activation function. It should work as follows.
<<>>=
relu <- function(x) max(0, x)
convolutional_layer(X, K, 100, relu)
@
\begin{exampleblock}{Bias as a threshold before ReLU}
For a filter response of 200 before adding the bias,
\[
\operatorname{ReLU}(200-150)=50,\qquad
\operatorname{ReLU}(200-370)=0.
\]
A more negative bias suppresses weaker responses. The fixed biases below illustrate this thresholding; a trained CNN learns its biases. Keep the supplied \texttt{mnist\_example} pixel scale of 0--255 here: rescaling pixels also changes the filter responses.
\end{exampleblock}

\item (2p) Run your convolutional layer on MNIST example digit 4 with bias -150. Visualize the feature map as you visualize the original image. What does the filter seem to capture?
\item (1p) Now transpose your filter and run your convolutional layer on MNIST example digit 4 with bias -150. Visualize the feature map. What does that transposed filter seem to capture?
\item (3p) As the last step in our convolutional layer, implement a two by two, two stride max-pooling layer. It should work as follows.
<<>>=
X <- mnist_example[["4"]][12:15,12:15]
maxpool_layer(X)
@
\item (1p) Put it all together and visualize the final pooled feature map.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
X <- mnist_example[["4"]]
output <- maxpool_layer(convolutional_layer(X, K, -370, relu))
@

\end{enumerate}

