\section{* Transfer learning using VGG16}\label{sec:transfer-learning}

This is the alternative VG option; see Section~\ref{sec:manual-convolution} for the choice and time-reporting instructions.

As the last step, we will look into using transfer learning as a quick way of improving the prediction accuracy on the cifar-10 dataset. Here Ch. 5.3 in \citet{chollet2018deep} or the \href{https://keras3.posit.co/articles/transfer_learning.html}{\texttt{keras3} transfer-learning guide} might be helpful.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Using \texttt{application\_vgg16()}, load the ImageNet-pretrained convolutional base with \texttt{include\_top = FALSE} and \texttt{input\_shape = c(32, 32, 3)}. Store it as \texttt{conv\_base}.

\emph{Hint!} With raw CIFAR images stored in \texttt{cifar10}, use \href{https://keras3.posit.co/reference/process_utils.html}{VGG16 preprocessing} as below. It converts RGB to BGR and subtracts the ImageNet channel means from the raw 0--255 values. This replaces the division by 255 used earlier. Apply the same preprocessing to any validation images.
<<vgg-setup-hint,echo=TRUE,eval=FALSE>>=
freeze_weights(conv_base) # Before compiling the full model
x_train_vgg <- application_preprocess_inputs(
  conv_base, cifar10$train$x)
x_test_vgg <- application_preprocess_inputs(
  conv_base, cifar10$test$x)
@

\item (2p) Add a flattening layer, a dense layer with 64 hidden units and ReLU activation, and a 10-class softmax output. Include the full model summary. Compare the number of trainable parameters in this dense top with the dense top of the CIFAR-10 baseline.
\item (2p) Reuse the CIFAR-10 training/validation split. Compile the full model after freezing the convolutional base, then train only the dense top on the preprocessed images for five epochs. Runtime depends on your hardware; roughly 200 seconds per epoch or more is possible. Time the first of these five epochs to estimate the remaining four. The first epoch may include additional setup time.
\item (2p) Report the VGG16 model's \textbf{test accuracy} and compare it with the \textbf{CIFAR-10 baseline} on the same held-out test images, using each model's required preprocessing.
\end{enumerate}

